WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.300
My name is Chris Adams.

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:02.800
I'm a globe maker

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.060
and I've been making globes

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.960
for the best part of fourteen years now.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.160
To make a globe you start off with a mould

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.960
and you make a very wet mix of plaster

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:33.360
and swirl it around to make sure there's a uniform coverage of plaster in the mould.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.520
When that is dry, you then make a second mix, a wet mix

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:41.520
and pour that in

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.800
and re-enforce it with paper.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.840
They used to use card or various other materials but I've found paper is the best

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.720
it's lightweight and still retains its strength.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.720
When that is dry, it's blown out of the mould

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.960
and the two halves are joined together.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.480
It's sanded down, rubbed down until they're very smooth

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.680
and given a final coat of shellac to
strengthen it and to seal it.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.200
At this stage the map will be applied.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.240
They're called gores, there are usually
twelve gores that go around a globe.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.800
They are stretched onto the globe ensuring
all lines match up so there's no

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.800
overlaps there's no under laps. Everything
should match up nicely.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.720
One of the things you have to bear in mind when applying a gore is the fact that the paper will stretch.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.240
It's designed to stretch two
percent in one direction.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:04.080
It has to be printed with that in mind, you have to reduce the the artwork two percent to allow for the stretch.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.840
I've been doing it for so long that it's quite easy for me gores

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:13.280
and make the lines match up and make sure they make sure the paper behaves as I want it to.

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:21.600
It's a skill that I've leaned and picked up, so I'd like to think I'm quite good these days.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.920
Once the gores have been applied they are given a seal

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:35.600
and then hand coloured, hand tinted and unusually given an age afterwards to make the globes look old.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:40.320
I prefer to think of it as giving them a head start in the ageing process.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:47.280
I try to remain true to the materials and methods of traditional globe making.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.120
Obviously, I improve where I can.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.120
A lot of the materials they used traditionally were animal based, which I like to move away from if I can.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.960
The plaster I use now is not dissimilar to
the plaster that they used but it's far

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.680
stronger and harder wearing.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.480
So it's little things that they would have incorporated as well, if they have the chance.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:17.600
Globes were traditionally educational which perhaps isn't the case these days

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.560
but I think a globe still has a role to play.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:28.480
It's nice to have something tangible in your hands to see exactly where we are in the world, where we'd like to go.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.360
You can spin a globe and see what's on the other side of it from where you are,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.400
which you can't necessarily do from a monitor or a book

